# The dashboard's trusted hop (LS-003 / D-1).
#
# WHAT THIS IS. `dashboard/src/middleware.ts` and
# `dashboard/src/lib/serverProxy.ts::requireTrustedHop` both refuse any
# `/api/**` request that does not carry `X-Omni-Trusted-Hop` equal to
# `OMNIAGENTOS_TRUSTED_HOP_SECRET`. Those guards are correct and fail closed by
# design. Nothing injected that header, so every public read and every
# authorized call 403'd and the dashboard was dark. THIS FILE is the missing
# injector — the deployment half of the fix. The guards are not weakened.
#
# OPERATORS AND LIVESIM BROWSE THIS PORT, NEVER :3003 DIRECTLY. A request that
# reaches Next.js without passing through here has no hop header and is refused,
# which is the entire point: "it came from loopback" is an argument for more
# scrutiny, not less (see the middleware.ts comment on why loopback is not
# trusted).
#
# BOTH header_up LINES BELOW ARE SECURITY CONTROLS. Each is a `Set`, and Go's
# http.Header.Set REPLACES every inbound value for that field name, in any
# casing, however many copies the client sent. That is what strips a forged
# header: a client that sends `X-Omni-Trusted-Hop: forged` (or a forged
# `Tailscale-User-Login`) has that value discarded here and replaced with this
# proxy's own. Verified by execution against caddy v2.11.4.
#
#   *** DO NOT ADD `header_up -X-Omni-Trusted-Hop` ABOVE THE Set. ***
#
# It reads like defence in depth and it is the opposite. Caddy applies Delete
# AFTER Set within one handler, so a `-Field` line DELETES the value this proxy
# just injected, the dashboard receives no header, and every request 403s —
# reintroducing exactly the outage this file exists to fix. Measured, not
# assumed. The Set alone is the strip.
#
# WHAT MUST NOT BE ADDED HERE: `Origin` and `Sec-Fetch-Site` must arrive from
# the browser untouched. The S-B1 CSRF layer reads them precisely because a
# hostile page's script cannot forge them; rewriting either here would forge
# them on that page's behalf and defeat the check the hop guard cannot make.
#
# Config: `OMNIAGENTOS_CADDY_PORT` (default 3013), `OMNIAGENTOS_DASH_PORT` (default 3003) are
# substituted when this file is parsed; `OMNIAGENTOS_TRUSTED_HOP_SECRET` and
# `OMNIAGENTOS_DASHBOARD_PRINCIPAL` are read at REQUEST time, so the secret is
# never baked into the parsed config and never appears in `caddy adapt` output.
# `scripts/launch-omniagentos.sh caddy` supplies all four.
{
	admin off
	auto_https off
	# Caddy's own diagnosability half: log the request, never the secret.
	log {
		format console
		level INFO
	}
}

# LOOPBACK ONLY — and `bind` is the ONLY thing that makes it so.
#
# A site address of `127.0.0.1:3013` does NOT restrict the listener: in Caddy
# the host part of a site address is a Host-header MATCHER, and the server still
# binds every interface. Measured with lsof: without the `bind` line below caddy
# listens on `*:3013` and publishes this whole control plane — the session-token
# proxy included — to the LAN. The address here is therefore bare (match any
# Host, so `localhost:3013` and `127.0.0.1:3013` both work) and `bind` does the
# actual confinement. `::1` is listed too because a browser resolving
# "localhost" commonly prefers IPv6.
:{$OMNIAGENTOS_CADDY_PORT:3013} {
	bind 127.0.0.1 ::1

	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:{$OMNIAGENTOS_DASH_PORT:3003} {
		# The trusted-hop assertion. Strips any inbound copy (Set replaces) and
		# injects this proxy's secret. If the variable is unset, this injects an
		# EMPTY value: the dashboard then logs `reason=hop_header_empty` and
		# refuses, which is fail-closed and names the misconfiguration. The
		# launcher refuses to start caddy without the secret so that branch
		# should be unreachable in a supervised start.
		header_up X-Omni-Trusted-Hop {env.OMNIAGENTOS_TRUSTED_HOP_SECRET}

		# The principal. `app/api/auth/login/route.ts` MINTS a signed 8-hour
		# browser credential out of this header, so it is a signing oracle for
		# whoever controls the value. Before this file existed nothing had a
		# valid hop header, so it could not be reached; now that this proxy
		# vouches for the hop, an un-stripped `Tailscale-User-Login` would let
		# any caller name themselves an arbitrary principal. Set — never pass
		# through. Unset variable => empty => login route 403s (reads still
		# work), which is the correct fail-closed default.
		header_up Tailscale-User-Login {env.OMNIAGENTOS_DASHBOARD_PRINCIPAL}
	}
}
